      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "Add customizable provider confetti palettes",
        "body": "# Provider-branded celebrations\r\n\r\nCodexBar now uses a small, provider-specific color palette when it celebrates a usage reset.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## The Problem\r\n\r\nI liked CodexBar’s reset confetti, but every provider used a newly randomized rainbow. A Claude reset looked essentially the same as a Codex, OpenAI, or Deepgram reset.\r\n\r\nI wanted each celebration to feel connected to the provider while keeping configuration optional.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Root Cause\r\n\r\nAfter I brought the issue to Codex, we traced the reset path together. <code>ScreenConfettiOverlayController</code> generated six random colors every time it ran. <code>ProviderBranding</code> stored one display color for each provider, but it had no provider-specific confetti palette.\r\n\r\nThe underlying Vortex animation already accepted explicit colors. The missing pieces were the palette data and the wiring from the provider descriptor to the overlay.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Scoping and Blast Radius\r\n\r\nWe considered three approaches: keep one shared palette, add a user-facing color picker, or assign a researched palette to every provider automatically. We chose the provider-palette approach because it creates a recognizable result with no required setup; users can optionally override it locally.\r\n\r\nThe functional change stays inside the existing reset celebration path and its optional local palette-editor path. It does not change usage calculations, reset detection, provider fetching, authentication, refresh schedules, or the setting that enables confetti.\r\n\r\nThe larger-looking part of the diff is mechanical: all 59 provider descriptors need to supply the new required palette.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Implementation\r\n\r\nWe researched all 59 providers individually, using the provider’s exact identity. Official logos and brand material were preferred; in-product or terminal colors were used only when clearer brand guidance was unavailable. The two providers added to main after the original implementation were reviewed with the same methodology.\r\n\r\n- <code>ProviderBranding.confettiPalette</code> requires every provider descriptor to supply two or three colors.\r\n- When a reset occurs, <code>playLimitResetConfetti</code> resolves the provider and passes its palette into the existing Vortex animation.\r\n- Tests cover default-palette behavior, local override persistence, and malformed or incomplete user input.\r\n- Each provider’s default palette sits beside its descriptor, keeping future additions explicit and small.\r\n\r\nThe current branch keeps the same split: provider-specific palettes are in <a href=\"https://github.com/steipete/CodexBar/commit/433ea2a0578c01416d12003b9fbb93bde375f12a\">commit <code>433ea2a</code></a>, and local palette preferences are in <a href=\"https://github.com/steipete/CodexBar/commit/ce3045ce82bfa4dc80b08be001638b8a2541c3c3\"><code>ce3045ce</code></a>.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## User Acceptance Testing\r\n\r\nDave ran the new build as his normal CodexBar for two days. Branded confetti worked as expected, and he reported no new issues.\r\n\r\nWe also triggered representative warm, cool, monochrome, and neon palettes—Claude, Codex, OpenAI, and Deepgram—and checked them against both a neutral background and Dave’s normal desktop.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Outcome\r\n\r\nReset celebrations now identify the provider automatically while preserving the existing animation, placement, timing, click-through behavior, and user setting.\r\n\r\n### Neutral background\r\n\r\n<table>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td align=\"center\"><img width=\"390\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/42f09788-72fd-4bdb-aa07-b451057fd5b6\" alt=\"Claude-colored confetti playing over a neutral gray background\"><br><sub>Claude — terracotta, cream, and near-black</sub></td>\r\n    <td align=\"center\"><img width=\"390\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7dfa979c-0ba6-493e-b91e-455bc25bf4b1\" alt=\"Codex-colored confetti playing over a neutral gray background\"><br><sub>Codex — blue-violet and lavender</sub></td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td align=\"center\"><img width=\"390\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6c12cf0c-cbb6-468b-ae39-e62e09277cc3\" alt=\"OpenAI-colored confetti playing over a neutral gray background\"><br><sub>OpenAI — black, gray, and white</sub></td>\r\n    <td align=\"center\"><img width=\"390\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/411821e6-fdab-4cab-835f-31fa63465390\" alt=\"Deepgram-colored confetti playing over a neutral gray background\"><br><sub>Deepgram — green, blue, and near-black</sub></td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n### Normal desktop\r\n\r\n<table>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td align=\"center\"><img width=\"390\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3f10a418-44e5-4e64-96cc-165530c6e654\" alt=\"Claude-colored confetti playing over Dave's San Francisco desktop wallpaper\"><br><sub>Claude</sub></td>\r\n    <td align=\"center\"><img width=\"390\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b0659d1d-6688-4c8f-88d6-070ca531f1a8\" alt=\"Codex-colored confetti playing over Dave's San Francisco desktop wallpaper\"><br><sub>Codex</sub></td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td align=\"center\"><img width=\"390\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7ad53894-dd80-427e-a2fa-bfa0df3648ed\" alt=\"OpenAI-colored confetti playing over Dave's San Francisco desktop wallpaper\"><br><sub>OpenAI</sub></td>\r\n    <td align=\"center\"><img width=\"390\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a1cd2d67-3ada-4ae8-bd61-afc27859fbb9\" alt=\"Deepgram-colored confetti playing over Dave's San Francisco desktop wallpaper\"><br><sub>Deepgram</sub></td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\nEvery clip contains 65 frames and runs for 4.34 seconds. The desktop versions use fully opaque frames so the browser cannot expose a different page or wallpaper color between frames.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Remaining Scope\r\n\r\nNew providers need a two- or three-color palette in their descriptor when they are added.\r\n\r\nUsers may override defaults locally from the provider Settings page; this contribution deliberately keeps that editor lightweight and local-only.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nCodexBar contribution notes prepared for the provider-branded celebrations pull request.\n",
